WEBVTT

00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:18,010
My ferritin is 37, and total iron is 107 in a blood test on February this year.

00:00:18,010 --> 00:00:22,410
You told me that my foot cramps may be from low iron.

00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:28,050
Do I have to take any kind of iron if yes, which one?

00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:41,450
I wouldn't give you, I'm just saying, I answered a question based upon what could cause cramping.

00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:49,450
So low minerals, low iron, those are the things that can be a cause of cramping.

00:00:49,450 --> 00:01:03,050
But I wouldn't as a person with cancer, I wouldn't use iron unless I had other signs of anemia.

00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:17,150
So I wouldn't recommend a cancer patient, use iron if they solely based upon their total iron

00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:20,550
and their total ferritin.

00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:27,150
Because honestly, your total iron of 107 is within normal range.

00:01:27,150 --> 00:01:35,550
So optimal range of total iron is like 85 to 130, so it's like perfect.

00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:40,350
Your ferritin is within normal range too.

00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:51,350
So I wouldn't based upon those lab findings give you total, I wouldn't give you iron.

00:01:51,350 --> 00:02:00,350
Your ferritin might be a little bit low based upon optimal range, but you're definitely in standard lab range with baritone.

00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:06,750
And then the other thing a person with cancer has to be careful with iron is that cancer had gobbled up iron.

00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:22,550
So we'd only give iron if the person showed signs of anemia, low hemoglobin, low hematocrit, low red blood cell count and low total iron.

00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:27,950
So we would jump to that.

00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:31,950
Yeah, I just, I wouldn't jump to that.

